## Diagnosis

Scoping the 37 findings to what actually sits on the failing path — `src/billing.ts`:

| Finding | Charge | Relevance |
|---|---|---|
| `crime_00013` | Temporal Recklessness — 7 clock reads @ L57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273 | **prime suspect** |
| `crime_00005` | God Function — `generateInvoice` 204 lines | hides the coupling |
| `crime_00025` / `crime_00034` | Test That Proves Nothing | why nobody caught it |
| `crime_00035` | God File — 311 lines | contributing |

One thing worth ruling out first: the report's *highest* agent-risk temporal cluster is in `src/date.ts` (`crime_00001` 8 clock reads, `crime_00010` mixed UTC/local, `crime_00012` timezone-unsafe parse, `crime_00018` DST-naive `+86400000`). That file is a textbook date-boundary minefield — but `src/billing.ts` **does not import it** (no import edge; `blast_radius: 0` on both). So it is not on the path of the failing test. Don't start there.

## Most likely cause

`crime_00013` names lines 57 and 92 in the same function. Read together:

```ts
// billing.ts:57
const createdAt = Date.now();
...
// billing.ts:92
const daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
if (daysSince > 365)      discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.10);
else if (daysSince > 180) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);
else if (daysSince >  90) discountCents = Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);
```

Two independent clock reads inside one function, feeding a **threshold cascade with strict `>` boundaries at 90 / 180 / 365 days**. A fixture built as "user created 90 days ago" — the natural way to write that test — lands exactly on the `Math.floor` boundary. Whether `daysSince` floors to `90` or `91` depends on the millisecond the test executes, which flips `discountCents` between `0` and `2%`, which changes `totalCents`, which fails the assertion. Same suite, same code, different result — that is the flake.

`crime_00015` corroborates the shape: `calculateRefundPreview` is a "safe-sounding name" that awaits I/O, so test setup around this module already has hidden ordering. And `crime_00013` flags the identical `daysSince` expression duplicated at line 273 in `recommendUpgrade` — fix one copy and the other keeps flaking.

Secondary (only if the failing assertion is on a *timestamp string* rather than an amount): `metadata.runAt` at L63 and the audit `ts` at L198 are separate `new Date().toISOString()` calls, so a test asserting they match will break across any second boundary.

## Fix

Take one clock reading at the boundary and extract the tier as a pure function. Addresses `crime_00013`'s `inject_clock` action and `crime_00005`'s `extract_function` action in one edit.

```ts
export type Clock = () => number;

const MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;

// Pure, boundary-testable. Note >= at the tier edges: "90 days old" now
// deterministically qualifies instead of depending on sub-ms rounding.
export function loyaltyDiscountCents(
  subtotal: number,
  plan: User["plan"],
  accountAgeMs: number,
): number {
  if (plan === "free") return 0;
  const days = Math.floor(accountAgeMs / MS_PER_DAY);
  if (days >= 365) return Math.round(subtotal * 0.10);
  if (days >= 180) return Math.round(subtotal * 0.05);
  if (days >=  90) return Math.round(subtotal * 0.02);
  return 0;
}

export function generateInvoice(
  user: User,
  items: InvoiceLine[],
  paymentMethod?: string,
  now: Clock = Date.now,          // injected; defaults preserve callers
): Invoice {
  const nowMs = now();            // ONE reading, used everywhere below
  const createdAt = nowMs;
  const runAt = new Date(nowMs).toISOString();
  ...
  const discountCents = loyaltyDiscountCents(subtotal, user.plan, nowMs - user.createdAt);
```

Then apply the same `now` parameter to `recommendUpgrade` (line 273) so the duplicated `daysSince` can't drift, and replace the remaining `Date.now()` reads at 198/238/250 with `nowMs` (the retry-loop pair at 238/250 is genuinely measuring elapsed time — keep those two as real clock reads, they're the one legitimate use).

Tests then pin the moment and assert the boundaries directly, which the current suite cannot do:

```ts
it.each([
  [89, 0], [90, 2], [179, 2], [180, 5], [364, 5], [365, 10],
])("day %i → %i%% loyalty discount", (days, pct) => {
  const now = () => Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);
  const user = { ...base, createdAt: now() - days * MS_PER_DAY };
  expect(generateInvoice(user, [line1000], undefined, now).discountCents)
    .toBe(Math.round(1000 * pct / 100));
});
```

Worth doing at the same time: `crime_00025` and `crime_00034` say `src/billing.test.ts` has one test with zero assertions and one with only `toBeTruthy()`. The suite currently proves nothing about invoice amounts, so the flake was free to sit there — the table test above is what should replace them.

I didn't apply any of this: `01-messy-ts-app` is the intentional-mess eval fixture, and editing it would move the detector baselines.
